---
// Changing crypto, written as "who is allowed to" rather than "where".
//
// A list of shops near a community goes stale in a season and cannot be
// verified at the counter. What can be verified is the licence: Dubai has a
// single regulator for virtual assets outside the DIFC, eight named
// activities, and a public register. That is the check a reader can perform
// before handing anyone money, which is more use than an address.
import VerticalLayout from "../../layouts/VerticalLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@dst/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Faq from "@dst/ui/Faq.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "Who regulates crypto in Dubai?",
    a: "The Virtual Assets Regulatory Authority, VARA — the sole authority for virtual assets across Dubai's free zones and mainland, with one carve-out: the <strong>Dubai International Financial Centre</strong>, which has its own regulator.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does a shop that swaps crypto for cash need a licence?",
    a: "Yes. Exchange Services and Broker-Dealer Services are two of the <strong>eight</strong> named activities, and a provider must hold a VARA licence before offering them in or from Dubai — whether the customer is a resident or abroad.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I check a provider?",
    a: "VARA publishes a public register of licensed providers. Checking it takes a minute and is the only step in this whole page that is entirely within your control.",
  },
  {
    q: "What are the eight activities?",
    a: "Advisory Services, Broker-Dealer Services, Custody Services, Exchange Services, Lending and Borrowing Services, Management and Investment Services, Transfer &amp; Settlement Services, and VA Issuance Category-1.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can one company hold several of them?",
    a: "Most of them, yes, aggregated under a single licence. <strong>Custody is the exception</strong>: a custodian must be a distinct legal entity with a standalone licence. Licensed providers are also barred from proprietary trading under the regulated licence — that needs a separate company.",
  },
];
---
<VerticalLayout
  vertical="riviera"
  current="/riviera/money/"
  prose
  title="Changing crypto in Dubai: who is actually licensed"
  description="Dubai has one regulator for virtual assets outside the DIFC, eight named activities that need a licence, and a public register. What to check before handing anyone money."
  image="/covers/rv-money.jpg"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Crypto nearby"
    title="Changing crypto in Dubai: who is actually licensed"
    image="/covers/rv-money.jpg"
    imageAlt="Cut-paper illustration: a square frame with a plain cream panel inside it, and a plain cream disc lying on the ground outside the frame."
    imageKind="generated"
  >
    <p class="lede">
      The useful question about changing crypto near home is not where the
      nearest counter is. It is whether that counter is allowed to be there —
      and in Dubai that has a checkable answer, because one authority licenses
      <strong>eight</strong> named activities and publishes who holds them.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>One regulator, one exception</h2>
    <p>
      The Virtual Assets Regulatory Authority was created by
      <strong>Law No. 4 of 2022</strong> and describes itself as the sole
      authority regulating virtual assets across Dubai's free zones and
      mainland, except within the jurisdiction of the Dubai International
      Financial Centre. Its Virtual Assets and Related Activities Regulations
      date from <strong>2023</strong>. So for anywhere in Dubai that is not
      the DIFC — MBR City included — there is exactly one licence to ask
      about, and exactly one register to look in.
    </p>

    <h2>The eight activities</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Advisory Services</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Broker-Dealer Services</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Custody Services</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Exchange Services</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Lending and Borrowing Services</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Management and Investment Services</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Transfer &amp; Settlement Services</strong></li>
      <li><strong>VA Issuance Category-1</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A provider seeking to offer any of these must apply for and receive a
      licence before it begins operating in or from the Emirate of Dubai —
      including where the customers are outside it. Swapping tokens for
      dirhams over a counter is not an unregulated corner of this list; it
      sits squarely inside Exchange Services and Broker-Dealer Services.
    </p>

    <h2>What the licence structure tells you</h2>
    <p>
      Two details in the rules are worth carrying into a conversation with a
      provider. First, activities can be aggregated under one licence, with
      <strong>one exception</strong>: custody must be segregated into a
      distinct legal entity with its own licence, and must demonstrate
      independent governance from any affiliate. Second, a licensed provider
      may not trade its own or its group's book under the regulated licence —
      proprietary trading requires a separate company.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both exist for the same reason, and it is the reason you care: they
      separate the money you hand over from the money the firm is playing
      with. A provider that cannot say which licence categories it holds has
      answered the question anyway.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout kind="warn">
    <p>
      This page describes who may lawfully provide these services, not how to
      obtain or move virtual assets, and it is not a recommendation of any
      provider. Licence status changes; the register is the current record and
      a page like this one is not. Check it yourself before any transaction,
      and treat an unlicensed counter as a counter with no recourse behind it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>
      How much of the community around you is finished is on
      <a href="/riviera/" title="How much of Azizi Riviera is built">the section front</a>,
      and what a landlord may add at renewal is on
      <a href="/riviera/rent/" title="What a landlord in Dubai may raise your rent by">the renting page</a>.
      If the interest is setting up rather than spending, company formation is
      at <a href="/llc/" title="Company formation in Dubai">the company section</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqItems} />

  <section class="container">
    <p class="source-note">
      The regulator's remit and the DIFC carve-out, Law No. 4 of 2022, the
      2023 regulations, the eight licensed activities, the custody
      segregation requirement, the prohibition on proprietary trading under a
      regulated licence, and the existence of a public register: Virtual
      Assets Regulatory Authority, licensed-activities and rulebook pages,
      read 22 September 2026. DST holds no virtual-asset licence and provides
      no virtual-asset service. Nothing here is legal or financial advice.
    </p>
  </section>
</VerticalLayout>
